.TH SET_ITERATOR_NEXT 3 "15 Mar 2023" "CB" "Library Functions"

.SH NAME
set_iterator_next \- Move to the next element in a set using an iterator

.SH SYNOPSIS
.IB #include <lib/set.h>

.IB int
.IB set_iterator_next(set_iterator_t* it);

.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBset_iterator_next\fR function moves to the next element in a set using an iterator. It takes one argument: the iterator. The function returns 1 if the iterator successfully moves to the next element, 0 if the iterator is invalid, and 3 if there are no more elements in the set.

.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.IB it
The iterator to move to the next element for.

.SH RETURN VALUE
The function returns 1 if the iterator successfully moves to the next element, 0 if the iterator is invalid, and 3 if there are no more elements in the set.

.SH EXAMPLES
The following example demonstrates how to use the \fBset_iterator_next\fR function:

.nf
#include <stdio.h>
#include <lib/set.h>

int main() {
    set_t set;
    set_iterator_t it;
    set_init(&set);

    set_inserts(&set, "apple");
    set_inserts(&set, "banana");
    set_inserts(&set, "cherry");

    if(set_iterator_init(&it, &set)) {
        printf("Iterator initialized successfully\n");
        while(set_iterator_next(&it)) {
            printf("Element: %.*s\n", (int)it.current->size, it.current->value);
        }
    } else {
        printf("Failed to initialize iterator\n");
    }

    set_free(&set);
    return 0;
}
.nef